25
00:01:15,250 --> 00:01:17,290
So let's get started.

26
00:01:17,290 --> 00:01:21,210
So today, I'll be talking about
from next-token prediction

27
00:01:21,210 --> 00:01:23,550
to next-generation
of intelligence.

28
00:01:23,550 --> 00:01:27,250
And what's the future of
pre-training in general?

29
00:01:27,250 --> 00:01:29,830
Before I get started,
this according to me,

30
00:01:29,830 --> 00:01:33,170
is the recipe to
build a SOTA LLM.

31
00:01:33,170 --> 00:01:35,890
And it has four key components.

32
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:37,550
You need smart data.

33
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:41,110
You need a lot of high-quality
data which is diverse.

34
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:44,890
You need to be able to do good
filtering, de-duplication,

35
00:01:44,890 --> 00:01:46,130
and so on.

36
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:48,370
You need smart architecture.

37
00:01:48,370 --> 00:01:50,730
So as you know,
architectures are evolving.

38
00:01:50,730 --> 00:01:54,290
From transformers, we now have
Mamba2 hybrid architectures

39
00:01:54,290 --> 00:01:55,330
and so on.

40
00:01:55,330 --> 00:01:59,010
You need smart algorithms.

41
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:02,250
How can you build
advanced training recipes?

42
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:05,530
And you, obviously,
need smart collaboration

43
00:02:05,530 --> 00:02:07,590
between pre-training,
post-training teams,

44
00:02:07,590 --> 00:02:11,930
research, and
engineering and so on.

45
00:02:11,930 --> 00:02:16,610
So, what is the work that
we have done in building

46
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:18,350
smart data for LLMs?

47
00:02:18,350 --> 00:02:21,810
I just want to give a
brief overview of that.

48
00:02:21,810 --> 00:02:24,690
So during my time
at NVIDIA, I was

49
00:02:24,690 --> 00:02:30,390
able to contribute towards MIND,
which is a synthetic dialogues.

50
00:02:30,390 --> 00:02:32,130
But they are all
related to math.

51
00:02:32,130 --> 00:02:35,890

52
00:02:35,890 --> 00:02:38,810
Nemotron-CC-Math,
which is common math

53
00:02:38,810 --> 00:02:42,370
that is passed from
Common Crawl documents.

54
00:02:42,370 --> 00:02:46,350
And all of this data, including
the Nemotron Nano 2 dataset,

55
00:02:46,350 --> 00:02:51,090
it's available in an open-source
manner on Hugging Face.

56
00:02:51,090 --> 00:02:53,670
We also developed
prismatic synthesis,

57
00:02:53,670 --> 00:02:57,290
which encourages diversity
in synthetic data generation.

58
00:02:57,290 --> 00:03:00,340
And Nemotron-CrossThink,
which was a dataset more

59
00:03:00,340 --> 00:03:04,280
geared towards reasoning
beyond math and code.

60
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:06,820
And this particular
Nemotron-CrossThink dataset

61
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:10,220
became one of the
most trending datasets

62
00:03:10,220 --> 00:03:15,260
from NVIDIA on Hugging Face
the week that it was released.

63
00:03:15,260 --> 00:03:18,980
But today, I'll be talking
about building smart algorithms

64
00:03:18,980 --> 00:03:20,340
for LLMs.

65
00:03:20,340 --> 00:03:24,900
And in particular, I want to
talk about three of our works--

66
00:03:24,900 --> 00:03:29,140
maximizing your data's
potential, front-loading

67
00:03:29,140 --> 00:03:33,860
reasoning, and RLP, which
is using reinforcement

68
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:37,220
as a pre-training objective.

69
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:40,260
So before I get started,
I want to give you

70
00:03:40,260 --> 00:03:43,835
an overview of what I'm
going to be talking about,

71
00:03:43,835 --> 00:03:45,460
and what are the key
components of what

72
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:47,460
I'm going to be talking about?

73
00:03:47,460 --> 00:03:50,700
So let's say there
are these four kids--

74
00:03:50,700 --> 00:03:53,980
Pascal, Volta,
Ampere, and Hopper.

75
00:03:53,980 --> 00:03:58,250
They all have access
to the exact same data,

76
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:01,450
the exact amount of
data, quality of data.

77
00:04:01,450 --> 00:04:03,310
Everything is same.

78
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:05,990
But what is different
for these four kids

79
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:08,750
is how they learn
from this data.

80
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:10,970
And there are three
key components there.

81
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:15,270
One is curriculum which is the
order in which you see the data.

82
00:04:15,270 --> 00:04:17,010
Second is front-loading
reasoning.

83
00:04:17,010 --> 00:04:20,430
What I mean by that
is all four of them

84
00:04:20,430 --> 00:04:24,430
have access to high-quality
reasoning data.

85
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:26,210
But, how do they use it?

86
00:04:26,210 --> 00:04:27,650
Do they use it early on?

87
00:04:27,650 --> 00:04:30,670
Or do they wait later
on in life to use it?

88
00:04:30,670 --> 00:04:32,870
And I like to give
an analogy over here

89
00:04:32,870 --> 00:04:35,870
with the American education
system, where there

90
00:04:35,870 --> 00:04:38,590
is this concept of
taking AP classes, which

91
00:04:38,590 --> 00:04:42,190
is college level classes
during your schooling years.

92
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:45,670
And the idea behind that
is that you will do well

93
00:04:45,670 --> 00:04:48,090
if you do-- if you take
those classes during school,

94
00:04:48,090 --> 00:04:49,850
then you'll not only
do well in school,

95
00:04:49,850 --> 00:04:51,630
but you'll also do
well in college.

96
00:04:51,630 --> 00:04:54,470
So that basically
captures the idea

97
00:04:54,470 --> 00:04:57,150
behind front-loading reasoning.

98
00:04:57,150 --> 00:05:00,590
And then finally, learning
through thinking, not just

99
00:05:00,590 --> 00:05:01,550
observing.

100
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:03,850
So these are the
three key strategies.

101
00:05:03,850 --> 00:05:06,390
And let's see how
these four kids learn

102
00:05:06,390 --> 00:05:08,950
using these three strategies.

103
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:14,150
So Pascal, unfortunately,
does not follow a curriculum.

104
00:05:14,150 --> 00:05:16,010
The data that's
available to him,

105
00:05:16,010 --> 00:05:17,770
he is just reading it randomly.

106
00:05:17,770 --> 00:05:21,950
He may read one text from
history, one very advanced math

107
00:05:21,950 --> 00:05:25,350
document, and then a
very basic math document.

108
00:05:25,350 --> 00:05:29,430
He does not utilize the
reasoning high-quality data

109
00:05:29,430 --> 00:05:30,610
that is available to him.

110
00:05:30,610 --> 00:05:32,350
He just never reads it.

111
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:35,790
And finally, he does not
learn through thinking.

112
00:05:35,790 --> 00:05:39,270
Volta, on the other hand,
follows a curriculum

113
00:05:39,270 --> 00:05:42,830
that is, she reads the documents
in a certain order and finds out

114
00:05:42,830 --> 00:05:45,790
that it's beneficial,
but she decides

115
00:05:45,790 --> 00:05:50,070
not to read the high-quality
documents available to her,

116
00:05:50,070 --> 00:05:53,270
and she also doesn't
learn through thinking.

117
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:58,560
Ampere follows a curriculum and
uses the reasoning documents

118
00:05:58,560 --> 00:06:00,880
that are available
to him, but he also

119
00:06:00,880 --> 00:06:02,640
doesn't learn through thinking.

120
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:06,740
And finally, Hopper learns
through a curriculum.

121
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:09,840
She also makes good use of
the high quality documents

122
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:13,480
that she has, and she also
learns through thinking

123
00:06:13,480 --> 00:06:15,100
and engaging with the material.

124
00:06:15,100 --> 00:06:17,560
She's not just
reading the material.

125
00:06:17,560 --> 00:06:21,460
So this is what we will go
over throughout the whole talk.

126
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:24,860
I'm going to be showing
comparisons between Pascal,

127
00:06:24,860 --> 00:06:26,460
Volta, Ampere, and Hopper.

128
00:06:26,460 --> 00:06:29,080

129
00:06:29,080 --> 00:06:32,840
So first, let's cover
maximize your data's potential

130
00:06:32,840 --> 00:06:38,320
and enhancing LLM accuracy
with two-phase pre-training.

131
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:40,880
So as you all know,
LLMs today are

132
00:06:40,880 --> 00:06:43,740
trained on trillions
and trillions of tokens.

133
00:06:43,740 --> 00:06:47,400
And they come from vast
sources, like all kinds

134
00:06:47,400 --> 00:06:50,980
of diverse sources that are
legal documents, books, papers,

135
00:06:50,980 --> 00:06:53,950
web crawl, which is
everything and anything.

136
00:06:53,950 --> 00:06:56,450
And you have math documents.

137
00:06:56,450 --> 00:07:00,330
And this is an analysis
done by Epoch AI, where

138
00:07:00,330 --> 00:07:04,430
it shows how much data is
consumed by the LLMs today.

139
00:07:04,430 --> 00:07:09,530
And it projects that by
somewhere around 2030,

140
00:07:09,530 --> 00:07:15,510
LLMs would consume more than
95% of human generated data.

141
00:07:15,510 --> 00:07:18,690
And the blue curve here is what
I want you to follow and see

142
00:07:18,690 --> 00:07:23,910
that in somewhere 2021,
when GPT-3 was trained,

143
00:07:23,910 --> 00:07:27,370
it was trained on hundreds
of billions of tokens.

144
00:07:27,370 --> 00:07:31,210
But Llama 3, which
was in late 2024,

145
00:07:31,210 --> 00:07:34,150
was trained on tens of
trillions of tokens.

146
00:07:34,150 --> 00:07:36,690
And that's where we are today.

147
00:07:36,690 --> 00:07:43,490
And soon we will consume all
of the human-generated data.

148
00:07:43,490 --> 00:07:49,570
So now that you have all these
different data sources, now

149
00:07:49,570 --> 00:07:52,450
there are two important
questions to answer.

150
00:07:52,450 --> 00:07:56,370
First is that, how do you weigh
these different data sources?

151
00:07:56,370 --> 00:08:00,410
Which is that, how do
you decide which document

152
00:08:00,410 --> 00:08:04,970
is a better quality document
and which document is not?

153
00:08:04,970 --> 00:08:08,710
And, how do you order these
different data sources?

154
00:08:08,710 --> 00:08:11,370
So again, like
curriculum, how do you

155
00:08:11,370 --> 00:08:13,570
know which document
to read first

156
00:08:13,570 --> 00:08:19,050
and which document to
read later on in life?

157
00:08:19,050 --> 00:08:22,190
So to create an optimal
blend or data mixture,

158
00:08:22,190 --> 00:08:26,290
we follow this simple pipeline
where you have all this data

159
00:08:26,290 --> 00:08:28,110
and you do quality estimations.

160
00:08:28,110 --> 00:08:30,810
So you build quality
classifiers and you

161
00:08:30,810 --> 00:08:33,730
try to estimate what's the
quality of each of your data

162
00:08:33,730 --> 00:08:34,530
source.

163
00:08:34,530 --> 00:08:38,010
And the idea then, when you're
creating your optimal data

164
00:08:38,010 --> 00:08:42,710
mixture, is that data sources
that have the same quality,

165
00:08:42,710 --> 00:08:45,470
they should be weighed
similarly in your data mixture.

166
00:08:45,470 --> 00:08:47,610
And higher quality
datasets must be

167
00:08:47,610 --> 00:08:53,700
weighed higher than medium
and low quality data sources.

168
00:08:53,700 --> 00:08:55,840
Next, you want to
do epoch estimation.

169
00:08:55,840 --> 00:08:59,580
This is basically estimating how
many repeats of a data source

170
00:08:59,580 --> 00:09:00,560
you want to see.

171
00:09:00,560 --> 00:09:04,220
So to get the most juice out of
your high quality data source

172
00:09:04,220 --> 00:09:06,140
especially, you
want to estimate,

173
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:08,180
what is the maximum
number of repeats

174
00:09:08,180 --> 00:09:11,100
you want to see of this
particular data source

175
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:13,420
before it starts giving
diminishing gains

176
00:09:13,420 --> 00:09:15,660
on your downstream tasks?

177
00:09:15,660 --> 00:09:17,560
So you'll estimate the quality.

178
00:09:17,560 --> 00:09:19,520
You'll estimate the
number of repeats.

179
00:09:19,520 --> 00:09:23,340
And based on this, you create
your blend, optimal blend

180
00:09:23,340 --> 00:09:27,260
or your optimal data mixture.

181
00:09:27,260 --> 00:09:29,220
And then for
curriculum, we follow

182
00:09:29,220 --> 00:09:32,900
a two-phase pre-training
approach, where in phase 1,

183
00:09:32,900 --> 00:09:36,440
the idea is to encourage
diversity in data.

184
00:09:36,440 --> 00:09:40,390
So you want to be able to
expose the model to as much data

185
00:09:40,390 --> 00:09:42,140
as you can and as
diverse data as you can,

186
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:46,340
where this is also mostly
the data mixture looks

187
00:09:46,340 --> 00:09:50,150
like it has a lot of web
crawl, including both medium

188
00:09:50,150 --> 00:09:51,950
and low quality crawl.

189
00:09:51,950 --> 00:09:55,790
You also show lower number of
epochs of high quality data

190
00:09:55,790 --> 00:09:57,310
sources at this time.

191
00:09:57,310 --> 00:10:00,030
And then in phase
2, the emphasis

192
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:02,430
is only on high quality
data, that is, only

193
00:10:02,430 --> 00:10:05,510
on higher number of epochs
of high quality data

194
00:10:05,510 --> 00:10:08,270
sources like math,
Wikipedia, code, and so on.

195
00:10:08,270 --> 00:10:09,970
So this is the
two-phase approach.

196
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:14,110
And this is what we follow
in our pre-training.

197
00:10:14,110 --> 00:10:17,530
And what are the baselines
for this approach?

198
00:10:17,530 --> 00:10:19,730
So first is natural
distribution.

199
00:10:19,730 --> 00:10:22,470
So you have three
axes-- quality, epochs,

200
00:10:22,470 --> 00:10:23,990
and order of the data.

201
00:10:23,990 --> 00:10:26,310
So in natural distribution,
it's basically

202
00:10:26,310 --> 00:10:28,710
you don't care what is
the quality of the data

203
00:10:28,710 --> 00:10:33,070
or, how many repeats should
I see of a particular data?

204
00:10:33,070 --> 00:10:34,630
In natural distribution,
the way you

205
00:10:34,630 --> 00:10:40,030
create the blend is that you
just draw samples from a data

206
00:10:40,030 --> 00:10:43,790
source, and they are equivalent
to how many tokens are

207
00:10:43,790 --> 00:10:45,170
available in that data source.

208
00:10:45,170 --> 00:10:49,010
So if you have a data source,
which is low in quality,

209
00:10:49,010 --> 00:10:53,190
but it is a huge
data source, it is

210
00:10:53,190 --> 00:10:55,150
very large in number
of tokens, then

211
00:10:55,150 --> 00:10:58,070
you will see more
of that data source

212
00:10:58,070 --> 00:11:01,390
just because it has more
tokens available in it.

213
00:11:01,390 --> 00:11:03,990
And in this baseline,
you also don't

214
00:11:03,990 --> 00:11:07,230
show any ordering of the data.

215
00:11:07,230 --> 00:11:11,670
The second is where you
create an optimal blend, that

216
00:11:11,670 --> 00:11:13,510
is now you have
figured out, what

217
00:11:13,510 --> 00:11:15,067
is a higher quality data source?

218
00:11:15,067 --> 00:11:16,650
And what is a low
quality data source?

219
00:11:16,650 --> 00:11:19,910
And you've created
a nice data mixture

220
00:11:19,910 --> 00:11:23,190
using this information
about the quality

221
00:11:23,190 --> 00:11:26,130
and information about how
many repeats you should see.

222
00:11:26,130 --> 00:11:29,350
But you're not really showing
it in any particular order

223
00:11:29,350 --> 00:11:31,290
to the model during
pre-training.

224
00:11:31,290 --> 00:11:33,430
You're just showing
it in a random order.

225
00:11:33,430 --> 00:11:35,790
And finally, our
two-phase approach

226
00:11:35,790 --> 00:11:38,290
where you create
the optimal blend,

227
00:11:38,290 --> 00:11:41,310
but you also make
sure that you show it

228
00:11:41,310 --> 00:11:44,030
in a particular order,
where in phase 2,

229
00:11:44,030 --> 00:11:47,150
you show more of the
high quality data source.

230
00:11:47,150 --> 00:11:51,400
So you take care of quality
epochs and order in this.

231
00:11:51,400 --> 00:11:54,960
And so this is the difference
between Pascal and Volta

232
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:57,680
that I was talking
about earlier, where

233
00:11:57,680 --> 00:12:01,620
Pascal neither did he
estimate quality or epochs.

234
00:12:01,620 --> 00:12:05,360
And he didn't follow any
particular order while learning.

235
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:08,720
Whereas Volta follows
a two-phase approach

236
00:12:08,720 --> 00:12:11,880
and creates an optimal
data mixture from the data

237
00:12:11,880 --> 00:12:13,800
that's available to her.

238
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:16,860
And if you do a
head-to-head comparison,

239
00:12:16,860 --> 00:12:21,760
then Volta is on average
17% better compared

240
00:12:21,760 --> 00:12:27,180
to Pascal and 3.4% better
compared to a random ordering.

241
00:12:27,180 --> 00:12:29,460
But you have an
optimal data mixture.

242
00:12:29,460 --> 00:12:32,640

243
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:36,380
So the next part of the talk
is front-loading reasoning.

244
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:38,800
And here we talk
about the synergy

245
00:12:38,800 --> 00:12:43,640
between pre-training
and post-training data.

246
00:12:43,640 --> 00:12:46,730
So the idea of
philosophy behind this

247
00:12:46,730 --> 00:12:50,590
is that today's LLMs follow
this particular pipeline.

248
00:12:50,590 --> 00:12:52,650
You have pre-training
where you try

249
00:12:52,650 --> 00:12:56,050
to learn about world
knowledge and facts.

250
00:12:56,050 --> 00:12:58,210
Supervised Fine
Tuning, SFT, where

251
00:12:58,210 --> 00:13:01,690
you are learning to mimic
reasoning like answers or you're

252
00:13:01,690 --> 00:13:04,010
basically learning
reasoning formatting.

253
00:13:04,010 --> 00:13:08,930
And in RL finally, you
actually learn about reasoning.

254
00:13:08,930 --> 00:13:10,850
And this in our
opinion where you're

255
00:13:10,850 --> 00:13:14,490
doing only general knowledge
during pre-training

256
00:13:14,490 --> 00:13:17,490
and in post-training is
where you add reasoning skill

257
00:13:17,490 --> 00:13:20,810
as a post hoc thing.

258
00:13:20,810 --> 00:13:25,330
That creates an unreasoning
foundation or a weak foundation.

259
00:13:25,330 --> 00:13:28,270
So what we propose is
to front-load reasoning,

260
00:13:28,270 --> 00:13:31,490
where in pre-training
you not only learn

261
00:13:31,490 --> 00:13:35,010
about general knowledge, but you
also learn about reasoning skill

262
00:13:35,010 --> 00:13:36,250
itself.

263
00:13:36,250 --> 00:13:39,850
And then in post-training,
you can amplify and refine

264
00:13:39,850 --> 00:13:40,850
this skill.

265
00:13:40,850 --> 00:13:44,810
And this, we believe, would
lead to stronger foundation

266
00:13:44,810 --> 00:13:49,210
for reasoning
models specifically.

267
00:13:49,210 --> 00:13:51,890
So what is the study
that we have done

268
00:13:51,890 --> 00:13:53,970
in front-loading reasoning?

269
00:13:53,970 --> 00:13:57,970
We basically systematically
inject reasoning style data

270
00:13:57,970 --> 00:14:01,510
at different phases of
the tree of the training,

271
00:14:01,510 --> 00:14:03,810
like pre-training SFT and so on.

272
00:14:03,810 --> 00:14:08,690
And we try to study, what is
the impact of adding reasoning

273
00:14:08,690 --> 00:14:11,170
data at these different phases?

274
00:14:11,170 --> 00:14:13,490
So this is the general
pipeline, that you have data,

275
00:14:13,490 --> 00:14:15,690
pre-training SFT, and then RL.

276
00:14:15,690 --> 00:14:19,210
And now you have access
to this reasoning data.

277
00:14:19,210 --> 00:14:22,570
And we categorize
this reasoning data

278
00:14:22,570 --> 00:14:26,090
along three axes, which
is diversity, quality,

279
00:14:26,090 --> 00:14:28,490
and the quantity of
the reasoning data.

280
00:14:28,490 --> 00:14:31,850
And, in this study,
what we do is

281
00:14:31,850 --> 00:14:35,330
we try to understand the
diversity, quality, and quantity

282
00:14:35,330 --> 00:14:37,130
of this reasoning
data to be added

283
00:14:37,130 --> 00:14:40,150
in pre-training versus in SFT.

284
00:14:40,150 --> 00:14:42,570
And what is the impact of it?

285
00:14:42,570 --> 00:14:46,780
So in the next few slides, what
I'm going to be talking about

286
00:14:46,780 --> 00:14:49,060
is that you have
this reasoning data.

287
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:50,560
And there are two conditions.

288
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:52,540
You either add it
in pre-training

289
00:14:52,540 --> 00:14:54,440
or you don't add
it in pre-training.

290
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:56,020
If you don't add
it in pre-training,

291
00:14:56,020 --> 00:14:58,660
then I'm going to call it
a no reason base, which

292
00:14:58,660 --> 00:15:01,700
is that in your base model
when you're pre-training it,

293
00:15:01,700 --> 00:15:04,100
you didn't see any
reasoning data.

294
00:15:04,100 --> 00:15:06,700
And the second is
the reason base,

295
00:15:06,700 --> 00:15:08,620
where in the
pre-training itself,

296
00:15:08,620 --> 00:15:12,500
you've seen some amount of this
reasoning data, some quantity,

297
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:15,460
some quality, or some diversity
of this reasoning data

298
00:15:15,460 --> 00:15:19,180
is added in pre-training.

299
00:15:19,180 --> 00:15:22,740
And here I just wanted
to show this slide

300
00:15:22,740 --> 00:15:25,420
on what evaluation
metrics we use

301
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:28,722
to evaluate pre-trained
models or post-trained models.

302
00:15:28,722 --> 00:15:30,180
And the reason I'm
showing you this

303
00:15:30,180 --> 00:15:32,940
is because there are
different benchmarks that

304
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:37,243
are used to evaluate base models
that is only pre-trained models.

305
00:15:37,243 --> 00:15:38,660
And there are
different benchmarks

306
00:15:38,660 --> 00:15:41,660
that are used to
evaluate models that have

307
00:15:41,660 --> 00:15:44,210
undergone SFT, RL, and so on.

308
00:15:44,210 --> 00:15:47,250
So when you see the numbers
in the next few slides,

309
00:15:47,250 --> 00:15:50,170
they may be different for
pre-training and post-training.

310
00:15:50,170 --> 00:15:52,710

311
00:15:52,710 --> 00:15:55,690
So, what did we learn
from this study?

312
00:15:55,690 --> 00:15:58,830
So first lesson is that
including reasoning data

313
00:15:58,830 --> 00:16:00,490
in pre-training is beneficial.

314
00:16:00,490 --> 00:16:04,470
So this is the comparison
between Volta and Ampere

315
00:16:04,470 --> 00:16:07,950
right after you finish
your pre-training.

316
00:16:07,950 --> 00:16:12,030
And so if you see, then
Ampere is doing 16%

317
00:16:12,030 --> 00:16:15,310
on average better
compared to Volta.

318
00:16:15,310 --> 00:16:16,870
That is the reasoning
base, that is

319
00:16:16,870 --> 00:16:20,850
adding Ampere saw this reasoning
data during pre-training,

320
00:16:20,850 --> 00:16:22,630
Volta did not.

321
00:16:22,630 --> 00:16:27,470
And ampere got
16% gain right out

322
00:16:27,470 --> 00:16:29,630
after the pre-training phase.

323
00:16:29,630 --> 00:16:32,150
And that shows us that
adding this reasoning data

324
00:16:32,150 --> 00:16:34,110
