00:26:01,890 --> 00:26:09,290
So you can design an ablation
study where you have a base data

496
00:26:09,290 --> 00:26:14,050
mixture, where you are just
using one repeat of that data,

497
00:26:14,050 --> 00:26:17,450
and then you keep increasing
it like to two repeats, four

498
00:26:17,450 --> 00:26:18,550
repeats, and so on.

499
00:26:18,550 --> 00:26:23,890
And as you increase it, the wait
that, that data source gets,

500
00:26:23,890 --> 00:26:24,870
that increases.

501
00:26:24,870 --> 00:26:28,170
But then you have to take
away that much weight

502
00:26:28,170 --> 00:26:31,420
from some other data source,
which is typically a low quality

503
00:26:31,420 --> 00:26:33,380
crawl or something like that.

504
00:26:33,380 --> 00:26:35,220
So you'll take
weight away from that

505
00:26:35,220 --> 00:26:37,300
and give it to this
data source and try

506
00:26:37,300 --> 00:26:42,060
to see if you do two repeats of
this, then what's the impact?

507
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:44,200
Four repeats of this,
then what's the impact?

508
00:26:44,200 --> 00:26:44,760
And so on.

509
00:26:44,760 --> 00:26:45,260
I see.

510
00:26:45,260 --> 00:26:49,620
And you do this for each
domain in your corpus?

511
00:26:49,620 --> 00:26:53,820
Yeah, we try to do it for
domain categories rather than

512
00:26:53,820 --> 00:26:55,500
individual data sources.

513
00:26:55,500 --> 00:26:57,260
Like so, for
example math, you'll

514
00:26:57,260 --> 00:26:59,740
just do it for all of
math rather than there are

515
00:26:59,740 --> 00:27:01,780
many data sources within math.

516
00:27:01,780 --> 00:27:02,280
I see.

517
00:27:02,280 --> 00:27:06,580

518
00:27:06,580 --> 00:27:09,860
Thank you so much.

519
00:27:09,860 --> 00:27:12,420
Out of curiosity, have you
tried the opposite approach

520
00:27:12,420 --> 00:27:17,100
of quality first and
diversity afterwards

521
00:27:17,100 --> 00:27:19,140
to see if-- because
you said first,

522
00:27:19,140 --> 00:27:22,680
we go for Monte-Carlo
Markov chain.

523
00:27:22,680 --> 00:27:25,620
You're doing exploration and
exploitation kind of thing.

524
00:27:25,620 --> 00:27:27,740
And you got better
results by doing diversity

525
00:27:27,740 --> 00:27:29,080
first and quality after?

526
00:27:29,080 --> 00:27:29,580
Yeah.

527
00:27:29,580 --> 00:27:33,950
So in the two-phase
approach, yeah, we first

528
00:27:33,950 --> 00:27:36,330
want to do diversity
and then quality.

529
00:27:36,330 --> 00:27:39,710
And then we did have an
ablation where we swapped that

530
00:27:39,710 --> 00:27:41,730
and it didn't give
as good results.

531
00:27:41,730 --> 00:27:42,230
Yeah.

532
00:27:42,230 --> 00:27:45,870

533
00:27:45,870 --> 00:27:47,870
So it's the opposite
of human beings, where

534
00:27:47,870 --> 00:27:50,950
if you train a child
well, after that,

535
00:27:50,950 --> 00:27:54,590
they can deal with rogue
behavior, whereas--

536
00:27:54,590 --> 00:27:57,110
in that sense,
exploration first seems

537
00:27:57,110 --> 00:27:59,230
to be doing the better job.

538
00:27:59,230 --> 00:27:59,850
Yeah.

539
00:27:59,850 --> 00:28:06,630

540
00:28:06,630 --> 00:28:07,150
Awesome.

541
00:28:07,150 --> 00:28:09,370
Then we can move
to the last part,

542
00:28:09,370 --> 00:28:14,790
which is using reinforcement
as a pre-training objective.

543
00:28:14,790 --> 00:28:17,910
So before I get started, I
just wanted to talk about,

544
00:28:17,910 --> 00:28:21,470
what was the motivation for
this particular approach?

545
00:28:21,470 --> 00:28:25,750
And I'd like to tell a story
of the tale of two learners.

546
00:28:25,750 --> 00:28:29,590
One is Leo who learned
by doing things.

547
00:28:29,590 --> 00:28:35,110
And second is Bolt who
learned by observing things.

548
00:28:35,110 --> 00:28:39,630
Now one day there was a
task to build a bridge,

549
00:28:39,630 --> 00:28:42,670
and you need to make
sure that this toy car is

550
00:28:42,670 --> 00:28:44,870
able to pass across the bridge.

551
00:28:44,870 --> 00:28:48,790
So Leo quickly grabs
a few stacks of blocks

552
00:28:48,790 --> 00:28:51,870
and puts them
together and creates

553
00:28:51,870 --> 00:28:55,050
this very simple bridge,
which is not at all fancy,

554
00:28:55,050 --> 00:28:59,310
but it works because this
car is actually able to pass.

555
00:28:59,310 --> 00:29:02,310
Bolt, on the other
hand, analyzes

556
00:29:02,310 --> 00:29:04,750
every bridge that
has ever been made

557
00:29:04,750 --> 00:29:06,910
and builds a
magnificent suspension

558
00:29:06,910 --> 00:29:11,150
bridge, which is very fancy
design, flawless precision,

559
00:29:11,150 --> 00:29:12,270
and so on.

560
00:29:12,270 --> 00:29:17,670
But it was so tiny that the toy
car could not pass through it.

561
00:29:17,670 --> 00:29:21,030
And so the lesson
here is about learning

562
00:29:21,030 --> 00:29:23,950
by doing and not just watching.

563
00:29:23,950 --> 00:29:28,000
And today's models, they learn
by watching text, that is just

564
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,160
predicting the next token.

565
00:29:30,160 --> 00:29:33,040
Whereas the
motivation behind RLP

566
00:29:33,040 --> 00:29:37,560
is to teach models to reason
through their own thoughts

567
00:29:37,560 --> 00:29:39,740
and not just by observing text.

568
00:29:39,740 --> 00:29:43,040

569
00:29:43,040 --> 00:29:45,860
So the problem with the
standard pre-training.

570
00:29:45,860 --> 00:29:48,680
So I've shown this
pipeline earlier

571
00:29:48,680 --> 00:29:53,000
that you have pre-training where
you're gathering world knowledge

572
00:29:53,000 --> 00:29:57,200
and you have SFT where you're
mimicking the reasoning format.

573
00:29:57,200 --> 00:29:59,940
And you're essentially
doing imitation learning,

574
00:29:59,940 --> 00:30:03,200
where if you predict
the next token, then

575
00:30:03,200 --> 00:30:05,800
you get some kind of a reward.

576
00:30:05,800 --> 00:30:09,880
Not RL reward, but yeah,
you get a yes or a no.

577
00:30:09,880 --> 00:30:12,480
And you're doing this
pattern matching thing.

578
00:30:12,480 --> 00:30:18,300
And only later during
the RLHF and RLVR phase,

579
00:30:18,300 --> 00:30:20,480
that's when you're
actually doing reasoning

580
00:30:20,480 --> 00:30:23,320
and you're giving the
ability to the model

581
00:30:23,320 --> 00:30:25,900
to do any kind of exploration.

582
00:30:25,900 --> 00:30:28,600
And that comes much later.

583
00:30:28,600 --> 00:30:32,480
And so reasoning is
used as an afterthought

584
00:30:32,480 --> 00:30:36,120
and added as a post hoc thing.

585
00:30:36,120 --> 00:30:39,080
And the questions we
want to answer is that,

586
00:30:39,080 --> 00:30:43,440
can reasoning be baked earlier
during pre-training itself?

587
00:30:43,440 --> 00:30:48,120
And then of course, do these
gains actually last or are they

588
00:30:48,120 --> 00:30:50,480
washed away after you
do the post-training?

589
00:30:50,480 --> 00:30:56,440
This is also very important
because earlier, I

590
00:30:56,440 --> 00:31:01,360
had shown this slide on
how the models today they

591
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:05,040
are consuming trillions
and trillions of tokens.

592
00:31:05,040 --> 00:31:09,480
And so we have hit a wall
where we are using the most

593
00:31:09,480 --> 00:31:11,640
number of tokens that we can.

594
00:31:11,640 --> 00:31:15,880
And so now the question
is, how do you use them

595
00:31:15,880 --> 00:31:17,580
effectively or efficiently?

596
00:31:17,580 --> 00:31:20,440
And so RLP is one
of the solutions

597
00:31:20,440 --> 00:31:26,050
for that as well where
maybe you need to learn to--

598
00:31:26,050 --> 00:31:30,490
or develop data-efficient
algorithms.

599
00:31:30,490 --> 00:31:34,490
And so what is the difference
between Vanilla pre-training

600
00:31:34,490 --> 00:31:36,410
and RLP pre-training?

601
00:31:36,410 --> 00:31:40,770
So you have this context
about photosynthesis

602
00:31:40,770 --> 00:31:43,910
which is the process
plants, algae,

603
00:31:43,910 --> 00:31:47,910
and some bacteria use to
make their own food using,

604
00:31:47,910 --> 00:31:49,970
and what's the next token?

605
00:31:49,970 --> 00:31:52,370
So in the standard
pre-training, you

606
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:56,330
would just predict the next
token given this context.

607
00:31:56,330 --> 00:31:59,210
And you would say it's sunlight.

608
00:31:59,210 --> 00:32:02,730
And that's just the
Vanilla pre-training way.

609
00:32:02,730 --> 00:32:07,250
But in RLP, you give it
an opportunity to think,

610
00:32:07,250 --> 00:32:08,870
an opportunity to explore.

611
00:32:08,870 --> 00:32:11,270
And it thinks that,
you know what?

612
00:32:11,270 --> 00:32:14,690
Photosynthesis actually
relies on solar energy.

613
00:32:14,690 --> 00:32:18,690
And so the next token
must be sunlight.

614
00:32:18,690 --> 00:32:21,770
So here you're basing
the probability

615
00:32:21,770 --> 00:32:25,360
of the next token given
not only the context,

616
00:32:25,360 --> 00:32:28,700
but the thought that was
generated by the model itself.

617
00:32:28,700 --> 00:32:32,300
And this is
reasoning-driven prediction.

618
00:32:32,300 --> 00:32:33,620
And this is RLP training.

619
00:32:33,620 --> 00:32:35,780
So the key difference
is that RLP

620
00:32:35,780 --> 00:32:39,940
produces an explicit reasoning
trace before predicting

621
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:41,500
the next token.

622
00:32:41,500 --> 00:32:45,420
And this makes the why of it
very visible and trainable

623
00:32:45,420 --> 00:32:49,060
and not just the final answer.

624
00:32:49,060 --> 00:32:52,520
So this is a brief
overview of RLP.

625
00:32:52,520 --> 00:32:57,580
And I'm going to go through each
of the components step-by-step.

626
00:32:57,580 --> 00:33:01,100
But before that, I wanted to
show you the prompt that we

627
00:33:01,100 --> 00:33:03,260
used to generate the thoughts.

628
00:33:03,260 --> 00:33:08,740
So where we ask the model to
specifically focus on next steps

629
00:33:08,740 --> 00:33:12,800
rather than jumping to this
final boxed answer and so on.

630
00:33:12,800 --> 00:33:17,140
And we also ask the model not to
restate any question or metadata

631
00:33:17,140 --> 00:33:20,940
commentary and so on.

632
00:33:20,940 --> 00:33:23,240
So first is, what is
our thought policy?

633
00:33:23,240 --> 00:33:24,520
What do I mean by that?

634
00:33:24,520 --> 00:33:28,520
And so you have the prompt that
I showed on the previous slide.

635
00:33:28,520 --> 00:33:31,260
You have the input,
which is the context.

636
00:33:31,260 --> 00:33:34,700
And this goes into
the thought policy.

637
00:33:34,700 --> 00:33:37,440
The thought policy
then does rollouts.

638
00:33:37,440 --> 00:33:41,180
It gets the opportunity to
explore different thoughts

639
00:33:41,180 --> 00:33:43,600
before it can predict
the next token.

640
00:33:43,600 --> 00:33:45,560
So the thought policy does both.

641
00:33:45,560 --> 00:33:49,620
It generates a thought and
then it generates a next token.

642
00:33:49,620 --> 00:33:53,180
Now this same input
context without the prompt

643
00:33:53,180 --> 00:33:56,800
is passed to the
no think baseline.

644
00:33:56,800 --> 00:34:00,060
This is our standard next
token prediction model.

645
00:34:00,060 --> 00:34:03,980
And given this input, it
calculates a probability

646
00:34:03,980 --> 00:34:06,380
distribution on your
vocabulary and predicts

647
00:34:06,380 --> 00:34:09,260
the next token sunlight.

648
00:34:09,260 --> 00:34:12,639
So from the thought
policy, we get p theta,

649
00:34:12,639 --> 00:34:19,139
which is probability of this
next token given the context, as

650
00:34:19,139 --> 00:34:23,830
well as the reasoning trace
or the thought tokens that

651
00:34:23,830 --> 00:34:26,590
were generated by the model.

652
00:34:26,590 --> 00:34:28,870
And from the no
think baseline, we

653
00:34:28,870 --> 00:34:32,429
get p phi which is the
probability of next token given

654
00:34:32,429 --> 00:34:33,630
the context.

655
00:34:33,630 --> 00:34:35,550
This is standard.

656
00:34:35,550 --> 00:34:38,830
So based on these two--
p theta and p phi,

657
00:34:38,830 --> 00:34:42,909
we calculate this information
gain-based reward.

658
00:34:42,909 --> 00:34:45,550
So this is the information
gain-based reward

659
00:34:45,550 --> 00:34:49,710
which is log of p theta
minus log of p phi.

660
00:34:49,710 --> 00:34:51,550
And what's
interesting about this

661
00:34:51,550 --> 00:34:54,590
is that if you
look at that term,

662
00:34:54,590 --> 00:34:58,270
then it's positive only
when your thoughts actually

663
00:34:58,270 --> 00:35:00,990
meaningfully
contribute to improving

664
00:35:00,990 --> 00:35:02,710
the next token prediction.

665
00:35:02,710 --> 00:35:07,430
So if the thought actually was
garbage or it didn't contribute,

666
00:35:07,430 --> 00:35:11,390
then your reward can
be negative or even 0.

667
00:35:11,390 --> 00:35:16,630
And as you can see, because
this is not your standard RLVR

668
00:35:16,630 --> 00:35:21,360
kind of framing, where it's
not a 0 or one that you

669
00:35:21,360 --> 00:35:25,640
get at the end, that
this reward is dense.

670
00:35:25,640 --> 00:35:28,400
It can have any value.

671
00:35:28,400 --> 00:35:33,200
And it can also be applied to
every position in the document

672
00:35:33,200 --> 00:35:38,680
without needing any
external selection process.

673
00:35:38,680 --> 00:35:41,420
So based on this
information gain,

674
00:35:41,420 --> 00:35:45,060
you get rewards for each of your
rollouts, which I mentioned.

675
00:35:45,060 --> 00:35:48,160
They are not binary,
they are dense.

676
00:35:48,160 --> 00:35:51,240
And finally, the last
component of this pipeline

677
00:35:51,240 --> 00:35:53,300
is exponential moving average.

678
00:35:53,300 --> 00:35:55,380
So what is this
No-Think baseline?

679
00:35:55,380 --> 00:35:57,800
This No-Think
baseline is basically

680
00:35:57,800 --> 00:35:59,700
updated but with a delay.

681
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:01,200
It is the same as
thought policy,

682
00:36:01,200 --> 00:36:05,360
but with a delay of
exponential moving average.

683
00:36:05,360 --> 00:36:10,680
And the reason we choose this
is because we want the No-Think

684
00:36:10,680 --> 00:36:13,280
baseline to be
current enough to be

685
00:36:13,280 --> 00:36:15,840
able to provide
informative comparisons

686
00:36:15,840 --> 00:36:18,280
during your training,
and it is also

687
00:36:18,280 --> 00:36:22,460
intentionally lagged so that
we can mitigate reward hacking.

688
00:36:22,460 --> 00:36:26,160

689
00:36:26,160 --> 00:36:29,940
So that's the whole
pipeline of RLP.

690
00:36:29,940 --> 00:36:31,940
And now to our experiments.

691
00:36:31,940 --> 00:36:35,240
So the question is, can you
improve the reasoning ability

692
00:36:35,240 --> 00:36:39,360
of a base model without
any task-specific tuning?

693
00:36:39,360 --> 00:36:45,340
So over here we use the
Qwen 1.7B-Base model,

694
00:36:45,340 --> 00:36:47,040
the final checkpoint.

695
00:36:47,040 --> 00:36:51,620
And we do RLP training
for one billion tokens.

696
00:36:51,620 --> 00:36:53,780
And we use general
pretraining corpora.

697
00:36:53,780 --> 00:36:57,880
So now over here, we don't use
any reasoning-specific data

698
00:36:57,880 --> 00:36:58,500
or anything.

699
00:36:58,500 --> 00:37:00,400
You can use your web crawl.

700
00:37:00,400 --> 00:37:03,760
You can use books,
papers, whatever you want.

701
00:37:03,760 --> 00:37:08,300
And we show comparisons with
the baseline, of course.

702
00:37:08,300 --> 00:37:11,160
We also show comparisons
after doing post-training

703
00:37:11,160 --> 00:37:13,730
which is SFT and RLVR.

704
00:37:13,730 --> 00:37:16,700
And we also show an
additional baseline,

705
00:37:16,700 --> 00:37:22,430
which is that if you use 1
billion tokens to do RLP,

706
00:37:22,430 --> 00:37:25,590
then what if you use the
same 1 billion tokens,

707
00:37:25,590 --> 00:37:28,230
but do the next token
prediction loss?

708
00:37:28,230 --> 00:37:31,730
That is, you just continue the
pre-training of the base model.

709
00:37:31,730 --> 00:37:33,950
So we also show
comparisons with that.

710
00:37:33,950 --> 00:37:36,610

711
00:37:36,610 --> 00:37:38,850
And so here are the results.

712
00:37:38,850 --> 00:37:42,850
RLP does show significant
improvement on the Qwen

713
00:37:42,850 --> 00:37:44,970
31.7B-Base model.

714
00:37:44,970 --> 00:37:47,570
And this is actually
the final comparison

715
00:37:47,570 --> 00:37:51,170
between Ampere and Hopper.

716
00:37:51,170 --> 00:37:54,930
And so when you do the base
model comparison, which

717
00:37:54,930 --> 00:37:57,490
is the bar charts
on the left, RLP

718
00:37:57,490 --> 00:38:01,990
does outperform the
base model by 19%.

719
00:38:01,990 --> 00:38:06,370
And if you use those same tokens
to do next-token prediction,

720
00:38:06,370 --> 00:38:11,270
then it still does better
than that by 17% on average.

721
00:38:11,270 --> 00:38:14,330

722
00:38:14,330 --> 00:38:16,890
And then when you do
identical post-training

723
00:38:16,890 --> 00:38:21,540
which is SFT and RL, the
gains by RLP, the compound.

724
00:38:21,540 --> 00:38:28,580
And Hopper does 8% better
relatively compared to Ampere.

725
00:38:28,580 --> 00:38:31,940
So here the lesson
is that RLP is

726
00:38:31,940 --> 00:38:35,020
able to establish robust
reasoning foundations

727
00:38:35,020 --> 00:38:37,820
during pre-training
that are not washed away

728
00:38:37,820 --> 00:38:39,460
by downstream alignment.

729
00:38:39,460 --> 00:38:42,240
After doing SFT and RL,
they are not washed away.

730
00:38:42,240 --> 00:38:45,500

731
00:38:45,500 --> 00:38:49,020
So in the previous slide, I
showed token-matched comparison,

732
00:38:49,020 --> 00:38:51,860
that if you do 1
billion tokens on RLP

733
00:38:51,860 --> 00:38:55,580
LP versus 1 billion tokens on
next token prediction, then

734
00:38:55,580 --> 00:38:58,440
you see improvements with RLP.

735
00:38:58,440 --> 00:39:00,620
But the question is,
would these gains

736
00:39:00,620 --> 00:39:04,580
sustain when you do compute
equivalent baselines?

737
00:39:04,580 --> 00:39:08,580
What I mean by that is RL
typically takes more compute

738
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:10,660
compared to
next-token prediction

739
00:39:10,660 --> 00:39:13,260
because you need to do
rollouts and you have

740
00:39:13,260 --> 00:39:14,920
to do n number of rollouts.

741
00:39:14,920 --> 00:39:18,900
You need to do completions to
generate this thought and so on.

742
00:39:18,900 --> 00:39:21,540
So we calculate the
number of flops needed

743
00:39:21,540 --> 00:39:24,460
using this equation here.

744
00:39:24,460 --> 00:39:31,100
And now if I train RLP
with 170 million tokens,

745
00:39:31,100 --> 00:39:33,680
